{"authored" [{"question" "q-353183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in [1,2].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/state.json" "file_sha256" "194be78e143009cec8748b9e74a9261c7ce81f1eebffce1410fdb84dfa3be862" "basis" 1302 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x),$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+16}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]-[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/state.json" "file_sha256" "194be78e143009cec8748b9e74a9261c7ce81f1eebffce1410fdb84dfa3be862" "basis" 1302 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4262bf588e027a0d6e7f55fddfebd4dbac0c3fff95882947208ffba7b7cbd297" "basis" 1302 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353183" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/state.json" "file_sha256" "194be78e143009cec8748b9e74a9261c7ce81f1eebffce1410fdb84dfa3be862" "basis" 1302 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-353183" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{8}+\\ln(2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/state.json" "file_sha256" "194be78e143009cec8748b9e74a9261c7ce81f1eebffce1410fdb84dfa3be862" "basis" 1302 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038326/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-5983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{2}{3}{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [1,4].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-5983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3/2},$ $a=1,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{({x}^{2}+1)}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x=2x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[2x\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=2{x}^{2}+1$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+x)|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2}{3}(4)^{3}+(4)]-[\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3}+(1)] \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-5983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "37b72e28ffad66cad153b4a40654281df0f02639ead2f867ea84bc18623450d2"} {"question" "q-5983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-5983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "28"] ["math" "38"] ["math" "42"] ["math" "45"] ["math" "54"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-5983" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "45"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-15403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\frac{1}{9}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{3/2}$ on $[0,3]?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-15403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{9}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{9}(\\frac{3}{2})6{x}^{2}{(2{x}^{3}+1)}^{1/2}={x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{({x}^{2}\\sqrt{2{x}^{3}+1})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}(2{x}^{3}+1)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-15403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "37b72e28ffad66cad153b4a40654281df0f02639ead2f867ea84bc18623450d2"} {"question" "q-15403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-15403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{12}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{6}+{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{8}+2{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{4{x}^{6}+2{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-15403" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{x}^{7}+{x}^{4}+1}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-82619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-82619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{1}{1-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{1}{1-x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm (-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|)+C \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-82619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "37b72e28ffad66cad153b4a40654281df0f02639ead2f867ea84bc18623450d2"} {"question" "q-82619" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-82619" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{1+x}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{1-x}+\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+7"] ["math" "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-82619" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-122389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+400{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-122389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+400{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=400{x}^{6}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 20{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 20\\int {x}^{3}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm 5{x}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-5{x}^{4}+2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-122389" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "37b72e28ffad66cad153b4a40654281df0f02639ead2f867ea84bc18623450d2"} {"question" "q-122389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-122389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-2{x}^{2}+2"] ["math" "y=-5{x}^{3}+2"] ["math" "y=-5{x}^{4}+2"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{4}+2"] ["math" "y=-{x}^{4}+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-122389" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-5{x}^{4}+2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-353183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in [1,2].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-353183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x),$ $a=1,$ and $b=2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+16}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2]-[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-353183" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "37b72e28ffad66cad153b4a40654281df0f02639ead2f867ea84bc18623450d2"} {"question" "q-353183" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"} {"question" "q-353183" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{8}+\\ln(2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059355/state.json" "file_sha256" "d17a9a9ffaa853970ea9720e31c17fac31589e6cd79a0c106b0e6ee06fc864cd"}] "usage" {"q-5983" [] "q-353183" []} "basis" 1892 "retractions" [[17592186083739 :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y = \\frac{2}{3}(x^{2} + 1)^{3/2}$ for $x ∈ [1, 4]$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186081801 17592186081802 17592186081803 17592186081804 17592186081805 17592186298887] "immutable_fields" [[17592186081800 :answer-field/correct] [17592186081800 :answer-field/id] [17592186081800 :answer-field/key] [17592186081800 :answer-field/type] [17592186298886 :answer-field/correct] [17592186298886 :answer-field/id] [17592186298886 :answer-field/key] [17592186298886 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
